1
00:00:01,120 --> 00:00:08,720
Welcome to the Subclub Podcast, a show dedicated to the best practices for building and growing app businesses.

2
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:19,280
We sit down with the entrepreneurs, investors and builders behind the most successful apps in the world to learn from their successes and failures.

3
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:22,480
Subclub is brought to you by RevenueCat.

4
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:32,720
Thousands of the world's best apps trust RevenueCat to power in app purchases, manage customers, and grow revenue across iOS, Android, and the web.

5
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:38,240
You can learn more at revenuecat dot com dot Let's get into the show.

6
00:00:40,880 --> 00:00:42,800
Hello, I'm your host David Barnard.

7
00:00:43,280 --> 00:00:48,880
Today's conversation is shorter than usual and will be featured in RevenueCat State of Subscription Apps report.

8
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:56,640
Each episode of this series will explore one crucial metric and share actionable insights from top subscription app operators.

9
00:00:57,200 --> 00:01:00,960
With me today, Lucas Level, VP of Product at Paddle.

10
00:01:01,360 --> 00:01:16,640
On the podcast, I talk with Lucas about maximizing success with web subscriptions, how web payment flexibility enables better user experiences, and why making it hard to cancel can ultimately hurt your business more than it helps.

11
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:20,080
Hey, Lucas, thanks so much for joiningining me on the podcast today.

12
00:01:20,320 --> 00:01:21,440
Thanks so much for having me, David.

13
00:01:21,840 --> 00:01:22,480
Looking forward to it.

14
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:31,680
So this is the first year RevenueCat has ever worked with partners to include additional data in the status of subscription apps report.

15
00:01:32,080 --> 00:01:37,200
And I actually haven't looked at this data yet, but Paddle has shared some web data that will be in the report.

16
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:51,280
And so I didn't want to talk through the data today, but I know you personally and the Paddle team work with a ton of apps on web to app and, you know, being successful on web payments outside of the app stores.

17
00:01:51,920 --> 00:02:00,160
So let's just kick it off with some of the top recommendations and what you see working on the web for subscription apps.

18
00:02:00,560 --> 00:02:01,280
Yeah, sure, sure.

19
00:02:01,440 --> 00:02:04,480
So I guess from our vantage point, we're really excited about web to app.

20
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:07,440
So Paddle is an end to end billing and payments platform on the web.

21
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:13,120
And we're working with lots of mobile app companies on their web revenue channels and how to best deploy them.

22
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:21,600
And we've seen, as you pointed out, and some of this data will be shared in the report, a big increase in mobile app companies who are looking at the web as an opportunity to acquire customers.

23
00:02:22,160 --> 00:02:22,240
Yeah.

24
00:02:22,880 --> 00:02:28,800
I feel like that's all I've been hearing about the last two or three years is like web, web, web, web, like how do we get web onboard working?

25
00:02:28,880 --> 00:02:29,600
And yeah.

26
00:02:30,080 --> 00:02:31,200
Yeah, I think it's really interesting.

27
00:02:31,360 --> 00:02:33,280
I think there are some key reasons why that's happening.

28
00:02:33,440 --> 00:02:39,200
And I won't, I won't bore everyone with that around the type of attribution and perhaps some gross margin efficiency around cost.

29
00:02:39,520 --> 00:02:55,600
But I think a lot of people are coming to the web and realizing that there are actually other advantages to the web around expanding your audience types, being able to reach users who are more present on the web and might not be searching for apps in the app store, and also leveraging a lot of the billing flexibility that you get on the web.

30
00:02:55,840 --> 00:03:04,160
So web billing has been around for a long time and it can provide a lot of flexibility and tools that you can use to your advantage on the web, which I think is really interesting.

31
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:09,520
So the way I think about it is very much, it's not just a cost saving opportunity, it's actually a growth opportunity.

32
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:20,960
There's a lot of opportunity on the web in terms of deploying different billing models and deploying different types of processes pricing, experimentation and AB testing in a way that that you're a little bit restricted with on the app stores.

33
00:03:21,280 --> 00:03:32,960
So I think in terms of like how to best think about it, I think the starting point for me would be thinking about like why people will visit your website on the web or how are people going to come to you on the web.

34
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:44,640
We see companies who are driving traffic to the web through content marketing and using content marketing as a landing point and then converting users into sort of paid users on the web and then pushing them to the app there.

35
00:03:45,040 --> 00:03:47,440
But we're also seeing a lot of companies deploy web funnels.

36
00:03:47,520 --> 00:03:54,400
And I think especially for for mobile apps that have a pretty large component of personalization, we're seeing web funnels be.

37
00:03:54,960 --> 00:03:58,240
a really effective way of driving traffic to the web.

38
00:03:58,560 --> 00:04:01,040
So the classic categories there are health and fitness.

39
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:19,360
We're seeing a lot of web funnels and health and fitness education as well as language learning apps, wellness apps who are all using that effectively and introducing the web funnel off the back of a paid ad on a social media platform, for example, and seeing really good conversion rates and then also good renewal rates as well on those subscriptions too.

40
00:04:19,600 --> 00:04:31,120
So a lot of reasons to consider the web, but I think it's also how you build a really effective web channel is very much dependent on the type of mobile app that you are and the type of customers or the type of users that you're looking to acquire.

41
00:04:31,440 --> 00:04:31,440
Yeah.

42
00:04:31,760 --> 00:04:55,760
It does seem like and you kind of alluded to to it already is that apps that do kind of have a higher intent user coming in, health and fitness being something we talk about constantly is that someone looking to get fit and or lose weight or or kind of these other categories of health and wellness, there's like a very strong, deep personal motivation to do those things.

43
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:57,680
And then also a higher willingness to pay.

44
00:04:57,760 --> 00:04:59,920
I mean, people are willing to pay a lot for their health.

45
00:05:00,320 --> 00:05:07,680
They may be spending fifty, one hundred dollars a month on supplements and gym memberships, and it can easily add up to hundreds of dollars a month.

46
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:12,240
So these health and fitness apps are relatively cheap comparatively.

47
00:05:12,720 --> 00:05:14,240
And then education and other things.

48
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:20,480
I think there are a lot of categories where you do see the web work better and then maybe some categories where you don't.

49
00:05:20,720 --> 00:05:27,760
Any kind of insights of where you maybe have seen apps struggle and then any other categories that you think have worked especially well?

50
00:05:28,160 --> 00:05:29,680
Health and fitness is working really well.

51
00:05:29,920 --> 00:05:30,960
Wellness is working well.

52
00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:33,600
Education and language learning is working really well.

53
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:44,880
I think some areas that we're yet to see it really take off and I probably wouldn't position this as like these companies are struggling but rather they haven't shown as much intent yet is probably in like productivity is a good example.

54
00:05:45,280 --> 00:05:52,400
I think the lack of personalization around how you use the tool, for example a note taking tool or something like this is not as interesting.

55
00:05:52,560 --> 00:05:56,480
I think when you're thinking about a web funnel and building buying intent through a web funnel.

56
00:05:56,640 --> 00:05:57,760
So I think that's an example.

57
00:05:58,320 --> 00:05:59,600
Media, perhaps, is another example.

58
00:05:59,840 --> 00:06:03,760
I don't have a lot of great examples around who it's not working for.

59
00:06:03,920 --> 00:06:06,960
I think it's more what are the categories that are leading this trend.

60
00:06:07,120 --> 00:06:14,640
And I think there are other ways in which those categories that perhaps aren't investing in it as much to kind of start thinking about it in the future.

61
00:06:15,120 --> 00:06:21,760
But at the moment, it's very much the apps with a high component of personalization that are seeing really, really good results with web funnels.

62
00:06:22,080 --> 00:06:24,880
What are the things, some of the things that you see working really well?

63
00:06:25,120 --> 00:06:30,240
You keep talking about web funnels and we've talked about this a lot on the podcast in the past, different forms of online.

64
00:06:30,560 --> 00:06:33,040
onboarding, different forms of building up intent.

65
00:06:33,440 --> 00:06:34,640
What do you see working in that?

66
00:06:35,520 --> 00:06:40,080
Mobile app companies that are thinking about the journey end to end are seeing a lot of good results.

67
00:06:40,560 --> 00:06:50,240
So thinking deeply about the web funnel experience, doing a lot of experimentation on the paywall and then also taking that through to the checkout as well are seeing great results on conversion.

68
00:06:50,640 --> 00:06:57,680
And I think companies that are looking at those journeys and really thinking about how we can reduce friction in that journey are seeing a lot of success.

69
00:06:58,240 --> 00:07:06,240
And also understanding the routes that people are taking through the web funnels and how they can produce a buying signal and looking at that data really carefully, I think is good.

70
00:07:06,480 --> 00:07:19,920
You know, we very much think about your success of the on the web, if you had to boil it down to two key data points or two key numbers that matter, it's very much your conversion through the funnel and your renewal at the end of the first subscription cycle.

71
00:07:20,240 --> 00:07:27,520
So how many users get through the funnel and convert based on a certain amount of ad spend, how many of those converted users actually end up renewing?

72
00:07:27,920 --> 00:07:31,440
It's easy to get distracted by the former and not think deeply about the latter.

73
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:54,080
And I think the companies that are seeing a lot of success are thinking really deeply about retention and deploying retention tools and using things like cancellation flows and downgrade flows to reduce churn and to drive the renewal rate in the right direction to really boost and maximize the ROI of the web revenue channel that they're working on.

74
00:07:54,560 --> 00:07:57,600
So I do want to dive deeper into retention here in a bit.

75
00:07:57,840 --> 00:08:09,040
But before we do that, I wanted to talk a little bit about some of what you just mentioned is that there's so much more flexibility on the web with how you charge the business models and the ways you approach monetization.

76
00:08:09,840 --> 00:08:16,320
And one of the things I hear about constantly and have been frustrated with the App Store for years is that they don't offer like per seat licensing.

77
00:08:16,720 --> 00:08:17,840
B to B is really hard.

78
00:08:18,320 --> 00:08:28,320
If you have a great B to C company and someone comes and says, hey, we want to buy twenty licenses for all of our employees, it's like there's not really a great way to do to do that on the App Store.

79
00:08:28,480 --> 00:08:36,960
Even like in a B2C sense where an employee wants to use an app for work, but they have a personal App Store account.

80
00:08:37,200 --> 00:08:42,480
It's like they would have to pay for it with their personal credit card and then have this whole reimbursement process.

81
00:08:43,120 --> 00:08:48,960
And so the web does offer so much more flexibility in business model and monetization and how you do things.

82
00:08:49,280 --> 00:08:50,880
What are you seeing be successful there?

83
00:08:51,440 --> 00:09:05,680
I think it's interesting because to be honest, based on what we're seeing, and we're primarily working with B2C, so sort of consumer subscription apps at the moment, and most of them tend to be deploying quite similar models on the web as what they are on the app.

84
00:09:06,400 --> 00:09:15,040
But we are starting to see signs of some companies sort of better understanding the billing flexibility that the web provides and starting to tinker and play with some different types of models.

85
00:09:15,600 --> 00:09:20,160
To be honest, I think a lot of consumer apps don't know what they don't know.

86
00:09:20,480 --> 00:09:31,040
And so there's definitely an education piece on our part to sort of show them what's possible on the web and support them in exploring different pricing and packaging scenarios and how they can think about setting up their billing models.

87
00:09:31,440 --> 00:09:41,680
I also think we're going to go further and further in this direction because if you look at AI and how AI is starting to be embedded in different software products, that's really changing the way that we price as well.

88
00:09:41,920 --> 00:09:44,640
So a lot more consumption usage based pricing.

89
00:09:45,280 --> 00:09:51,680
Tying pricing to the value that you receive from a product as opposed to just the standard price, irrespective of how often you use it.

90
00:09:52,080 --> 00:09:58,880
I think we will start to see that roll into the consumer side a little bit more as consumers start to use more of these products.

91
00:09:59,280 --> 00:10:04,720
I think what we're seeing, at least from our vantage point, is sort of bringing over the simplicity of the App Store into the web.

92
00:10:05,200 --> 00:10:18,400
We are seeing more flexibility on the retention side, I think, but I think from the billing model and pricing model perspective, I think we've got a way to go before we see mobile apps really start to challenge some of those norms that have been ingrained in them based on some of the restrictions in app store billing.

93
00:10:18,800 --> 00:10:18,880
Yeah.

94
00:10:19,040 --> 00:10:27,440
I mean, that's part of the whole conversation of the last few years around web is that it is this kind of whole new opportunity that most apps aren't used to working.

95
00:10:27,600 --> 00:10:29,840
And so there is like this kind of exploration phase.

96
00:10:30,240 --> 00:10:30,720
And I agree.

97
00:10:30,800 --> 00:10:41,280
I mean, I think we're just going to see a lot more experimentation over time with those different kinds of pricing and packaging and B2B opportunities and company billing and stuff like that.

98
00:10:41,440 --> 00:10:47,120
So it's an interesting opportunity for folks to be exploring even if you're not seeing a ton of it yet.

99
00:10:47,760 --> 00:11:01,040
The other thing I do hear a lot about from a web billing perspective is winbacks, is that if you're going to offer a discounted subscription to try and win someone back, a great way to do that is to go ahead and send them to the web.

100
00:11:01,840 --> 00:11:08,160
It's a lot higher intent when you say, hey, 50% off or 25% off if you go to the web.

101
00:11:08,560 --> 00:11:10,560
Are you seeing those kinds of things being successful?

102
00:11:11,040 --> 00:11:11,280
We are.

103
00:11:11,440 --> 00:11:12,000
Yes, we are.

104
00:11:12,400 --> 00:11:19,120
We are seeing quite a number of examples of discounted pricing on the web and using discounted pricing in winback campaigns.

105
00:11:19,280 --> 00:11:31,920
As you mentioned, we're even seeing companies choose to offer standard pricing at a lower rate on the web, just taking into account the fact that, you know, the fee component is much smaller on the web and choosing to pass on some of those savings to the user.

106
00:11:32,400 --> 00:11:37,600
I think there are a lot of companies that are starting to dabble in that space and I think consumers will start to pick up on that.

107
00:11:37,680 --> 00:11:42,560
And I've known everyday people sort of pick up on the fact that, you know, buying X is cheaper on the web the web than it is on the app.

108
00:11:42,720 --> 00:11:43,520
So I'm going to go and do that.

109
00:11:43,760 --> 00:11:48,400
Or I'm going to cancel my subscription on the app and resubscribe on the web because of a cheaper price.

110
00:11:48,720 --> 00:11:50,480
There's some complexity around that, right?

111
00:11:50,880 --> 00:11:58,080
Around like when you can kind of link from the app stores, how you kind of build those mechanisms, but it's definitely possible and something we're definitely seeing.

112
00:11:58,400 --> 00:11:58,480
Yeah.

113
00:11:58,720 --> 00:12:07,600
And, you know, I don't know that everybody is fully aware of this because I still get questions almost on a monthly basis from people like, wait, can I actually send people to the web?

114
00:12:08,320 --> 00:12:13,040
And the basic answer is that you can't send people to the web from inside the app.

115
00:12:13,280 --> 00:12:21,520
Although there's even more flexibility now with both Apple and Google with special entitlements where you can actually in the app send to the web, but then you still have to payy commission.

116
00:12:21,840 --> 00:12:22,800
It's all a bit complex.

117
00:12:23,040 --> 00:12:43,200
But as of, I think, about two, two and a half years ago, there was a ruling, I think it was the Japan Fair Trade Commission that kind of pushed Apple on this where once you have someone's contact information, even if you collect it within the app, Apple tried for a while, like, hey, if you collect an email inside the app, you can't then use that email to send people to the web.

118
00:12:43,600 --> 00:12:46,080
But that's clearly very anti competitive.

119
00:12:46,240 --> 00:12:47,680
And so they backed off on that.

120
00:12:48,320 --> 00:12:54,480
And so any contact information that you collect within the app can be used to then push people to the web.

121
00:12:54,720 --> 00:13:00,080
So I've even seen apps where if during that onboarding flow inside the app, they do collect an email.

122
00:13:00,560 --> 00:13:02,720
and the user doesn't immediately subscribe.

123
00:13:03,440 --> 00:13:14,880
Then within minutes they're pushing an email and maybe it even drops down as a notification while they're still in your app saying, hey, come to the web and save, you know, thirty percent or forty percent or whatever that is.

124
00:13:15,120 --> 00:13:19,840
I mean, of course, Apple doesn't love that happens, but like that is okay now.

125
00:13:20,080 --> 00:13:23,360
Like they can't and have stated in the rules that that is okay.

126
00:13:24,160 --> 00:13:30,960
So it can be a really powerful tactic to nudge those users and using email and text or other ways to do that.

127
00:13:31,120 --> 00:13:36,560
I think push notifications are still under the rule of like that's kind of technically in their ecosystem.

128
00:13:36,880 --> 00:13:42,480
Although I do still see apps doing that on occasion using push notifications instead of email or text.

129
00:13:42,800 --> 00:13:50,560
But yeah, I think a lot of people are not aware of just how much they can use even those channels to push people to the web.

130
00:13:51,040 --> 00:13:56,400
And this is why we're seeing, this is why we're seeing, especially in gaming, a lot more account creation.

131
00:13:56,960 --> 00:14:00,160
So apps that typically didn't require account creation.

132
00:14:00,240 --> 00:14:10,720
So you're just in a game and you just sort of just play the game are now asking you to create an account, put in your name, put in your email address for the purpose of being able to retarget you in different ways through other channels that aren't directly the app.

133
00:14:11,120 --> 00:14:12,160
It makes sense in gaming, right?

134
00:14:12,480 --> 00:14:14,160
Because you're purchasing things on the fly.

135
00:14:14,400 --> 00:14:17,680
So if you're playing in an app and you're purchasing gems or tokens or.

136
00:14:17,680 --> 00:14:21,920
or tokens or swords or whatever, right, in game, like in game economy items.

137
00:14:22,160 --> 00:14:24,720
And it's not just a one-off purchase with a renewal at the start.

138
00:14:25,040 --> 00:14:31,360
There is a big drive to push them to a web store to make those purchases where they're offering bundled products at discounted prices.

139
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,240
But to do that, they need to be able to contact you through other means.

140
00:14:34,640 --> 00:14:38,400
And so we're seeing a lot of that through the evolution of the web store in gaming, right?

141
00:14:38,560 --> 00:14:43,520
The Epic Games store is a great example of a very successful strategy that is really working for Epic Games.

142
00:14:43,920 --> 00:14:45,440
And there are other great examples of that as well.

143
00:14:45,520 --> 00:14:47,680
But yeah, it's a brilliant point and you're spot on.

144
00:14:47,840 --> 00:14:49,120
Yeah, I mean, I haven't seen that.

145
00:14:49,440 --> 00:14:52,160
I've played Pokemon Go with my kids for years now.

146
00:14:52,480 --> 00:14:56,720
And I just got an email like a few weeks ago of web exclusive bundles that like.

147
00:14:56,800 --> 00:15:01,040
that really highlighted the deals that you can get on the web for Pokemon Go.

148
00:15:01,360 --> 00:15:03,360
So yeah, I've been seeing it more and more as well.

149
00:15:03,600 --> 00:15:09,600
So I did want to transition and start talking a little bit more about retention and churn mitigation on the web.

150
00:15:10,160 --> 00:15:20,160
I think it's no secret that some of the reasons some apps do see better retention on the web is that it is just harder to cancel.

151
00:15:20,400 --> 00:15:26,400
And I mean, famously, you know, New York Times and others have made you call to cancel if you have a web subscription.

152
00:15:26,800 --> 00:15:27,200
And it is.

153
00:15:27,440 --> 00:15:30,640
It is much easier to cancel on the app stores.

154
00:15:30,960 --> 00:15:34,000
And so some of the boosts that you're going to see on the web are just people.

155
00:15:34,480 --> 00:15:34,960
It's daunting.

156
00:15:35,440 --> 00:15:36,480
I'm going to have to go log in.

157
00:15:36,560 --> 00:15:39,520
I'm going to have to dig through settings and find a way to cancel.

158
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:47,200
But ultimately, you're not going to build a great business on people not being able to cancel, preventing them from canceling.

159
00:15:47,840 --> 00:15:50,240
Those are the people who are going to be frustrated with your brand.

160
00:15:50,480 --> 00:15:54,640
They're going to be telling their friends not to subscribe because they had a hard time unsubscribing.

161
00:15:54,880 --> 00:16:01,600
And so to build a great business, you don't want to put huge barriers in the way of canceling.

162
00:16:02,320 --> 00:16:05,280
You want users to be engaged with the app and actually getting value.

163
00:16:05,680 --> 00:16:15,360
So what are some of the ways that you've seen the web work well for retaining users and doing so in a way that actually is like consumer friendly.

164
00:16:15,840 --> 00:16:16,640
I think this is really important.

165
00:16:17,040 --> 00:16:17,920
And I actually agree with you.

166
00:16:18,080 --> 00:16:20,960
Like you can't build a sustainable business on dark UX patterns, right?

167
00:16:21,200 --> 00:16:22,080
We see that on both sides.

168
00:16:22,240 --> 00:16:24,640
We don't just see that on the cancellation side.

169
00:16:24,880 --> 00:16:29,760
You do see that on the front as well at the paywall around like transparency around pricing, right?

170
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:31,360
Because the web is much more flexible.

171
00:16:31,520 --> 00:16:39,760
You're not sort of under the guise of a governance structure like Apple, where you can push boundaries around that sort of thing and mislead consumers in certain ways.

172
00:16:39,840 --> 00:16:42,880
And there's just no way you're going to build a sustainable business out of that.

173
00:16:43,120 --> 00:16:44,800
So like we would never, ever encourage that.

174
00:16:44,960 --> 00:16:52,000
I think the part that is perhaps different on the web is tools available to you in order to prevent churn.

175
00:16:52,240 --> 00:16:53,280
There are a number of examples of this.

176
00:16:53,360 --> 00:17:00,480
And I think your point about like the FTC's new click to cancel rule is spot on and on the web companies need to be really aware of that.

177
00:17:00,960 --> 00:17:01,920
There are options for you.

178
00:17:02,160 --> 00:17:22,240
So when someone clicks a cancel button and it might be a one click cancel button, but you can present a cancellation flow which can ask some questions about why they're canceling and present some alternative options, you can build ways to trigger a downgrade flow or a pause flow instead of a cancel flow, which means you might be downgrading them from one plan to another, but you're not losing them entirely.

179
00:17:22,880 --> 00:17:29,440
You can also deploy reactivations and win back campaigns in different ways on the web, which I think is really interesting as well.

180
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,480
So I think the web just gives you more flexibility around.

181
00:17:33,200 --> 00:17:45,120
